# Round 13 — the memory lifecycle's consumer adequacy + the testing approach

**Scope:** two questions the D35 postmortem opened (GitHub issue #1:
every read surface dropped `Memory.content` with the whole gate matrix
green). (a) The memory lifecycle end-to-end — write, read, maintain,
consolidate, export/restore — audited for CONSUMER adequacy: for every
stage, who consumes the output, and is the consumer's need pinned by an
executable test or acceptance criterion, not merely by cross-surface
consistency. (b) The testing approach itself — where the suite proves
consistency but not adequacy, where additive-safe subset assertions
dominate, and which agent-facing payloads still lack a consumer
contract. Engine: two read-only scout passes over
`exocortex-client`/`exocortex-ops`/`exocortex-server`/`exocortex-dreams`
/`exocortex-ingest`/`xtask` (every claim below carries its file:line),
cross-checked against the playbook, the README tool table, the
agent-instructions PRD §11 block, and `docs/acceptance/section-23.tsv`.
Not in scope: the adapters (round-12 covered), live-backend legs
(FALKOR/REDIS/POSTGRES/GitHub/Linear URLs unset throughout — stated
plainly).

**Verdict: 8 findings — 3 verified-and-actionable (2 fixed in this
round's commits, 1 → D39), 3 recorded decisions (1 → D40), 2 clean.
D37 (ai1/S8 + consumer-contract tests) landed as this round's
companion before the review wrote this file, so the read surface it
audits was already pinned.**

## Part A — the lifecycle, stage by stage

### Write (`end_session`)

The ONLINE ack (`EndSessionAck`: accepted/rejected/rejections[
{draft_key, code, detail, correction}]/local_validation_failed/
unverified[]/similar_to[{draft_key, existing_memory_id,
existing_title, suggestion}]) is fully specified
(client/src/tools/end_session.rs:56-104) and pinned at the wire and
registry layers — ingest grouping tests assert `detail` content and
`suggestion` values (ingest/tests/grouping.rs:597-607), ops and HTTP
preflight assert code+detail+correction (ops/tests/parity.rs:1511-1516,
server/tests/http_parity.rs:725-727). **R13-1:** none of that is pinned
through the `exocortex.end_session` TOOL itself — the only stdio-level
rejection assertion checks a substring of the rendered JSON
(client/tests/backend_project_grant.rs:275-279, `"accepted":0`), and
the playbook's core promise "Rejections: read the `code` and `detail`,
fix, resubmit same turn" (playbook v1_0_0.md:47-48, 148-149) is
therefore unpinned on the surface agents actually call. The OFFLINE
ack is now pinned by ai1 (local_lsns, sync_pending, refusal naming the
offender). Disposition: **D39** — extend the consumer-contract harness
to the `--backend` path (the backend_project_grant in-process backend
node is the vehicle), pinning rejections code+detail+correction,
`local_validation_failed`, `unverified[]`, and the `similar_to` shape
through the real tool. Sub-finding: the `distinct` suggestion value is
promised (PRD §4.10b) and pinned NOWHERE (wire pins `duplicate` and
`contradicts` only) — D39 covers it.

### Read (`search_memories` / `get_memory` / `find_related`)

Content + label + citable ids + supersession-through-the-tool are now
pinned end-to-end (D35 + ai1). **R13-2 (recorded decision):**
`MemoryJson` deliberately does not expose `tags`, `summary`,
timestamps, or the usage-context block; nothing promises them (the
README's "titles/tags" describes the search INDEX, not the payload),
and search matching over tags is separately pinned. Decision: not
exposed in v1; revisit on demonstrated consumer demand (a Pickle-Rick
lesson-injection need for tags would be that demand). **R13-3:** the
`unverified[]` list is promised to agents ("Both tools name those in
an `unverified` list", v1_0_0.md:204) and pinned by no test anywhere —
it rides D39's online contract.

### Maintain (promote/resolve/retract/accept)

All four backend-HTTP ops return outputs whose consumer-relevant
fields are pinned with VALUE assertions (not shape): audit_lsn > 0 and
echoed resolution/superseded/edge ids through both ops and HTTP
(ops/tests/parity.rs:211, 329-330, 1141, 1701-1703;
server/tests/http_parity.rs:536-595). The full discovery consumer
chain (list → issue → accept) is value-pinned end-to-end
(parity.rs:413-510). **No findings.**

### Consolidate (Dreams)

Discovery proposals are value-pinned to their consumer (quality 0.6/
0.9, via_types sentinels, visibility — parity.rs:451-470,
dreams/tests/tenancy_regions.rs:234-237). **R13-4:** abstraction rows
(carrier `General`, title "Abstraction of N <Type>", content = first
eight member titles) reach the READ path as ordinary rows, and no test
pins that an abstraction is retrievable through `search_memories` or
`find_related` — only internal id/timestamp pins exist
(dreams/tests/abstractions.rs:198, 220-243). Disposition: **D40**
(Proposed; needs a Dreams cycle and a read through the served snapshot
in one test — the dreams `testing` feature is the vehicle).

### Export / restore

The server-side org backup is byte-faithful modulo LSN
(org_backup.rs:240-244) — content survival pinned. **R13-5:** the
CLIENT WAL restore path verifies survival through an (id, title)
projection only (client/tests/backup_restore.rs:113-130) — content
survival through the tool a CLI user actually runs was unpinned.
Disposition: fixed in this round — `hits_of` now carries content and
the round-trip asserts the body byte-equal after wipe+restore.

## Part B — the testing approach

**R13-6 (the structural finding, verified):** every parity/identity
leg in the workspace compares a surface against ITSELF. The stdio IN10
leg's "registry side" calls the identical fn-pointer the MCP tool
dispatches (client/src/mcp.rs:218; test at stdio_smoke.rs:732-768);
http_parity's "typed handler" leg is the same `entry.handler` the
route closure runs (server/src/http_bind.rs:320; http_parity.rs:349,
463-464, 597-602); ops' both-surfaces leg compares the generated
wrapper with the op it wraps (ops/src/lib.rs:265-270; parity.rs:144-
165); artifact-equivalence's console claim "MCP result == registry
result" re-runs the same-handler stdio leg. These pin transport
wrapping (their purpose — CR-9 remains real) and CANNOT catch a
shared-projection omission: D35 shipped exactly that way. The only
genuine two-implementation payload comparison in the client/ops/server
suites is the grouping-node builder pair
(standalone_readback.rs:478-512). Disposition: adequacy moves to
consumer contracts (the ai1 pattern — real binary, documented
promises); parity legs keep their transport role. D39 extends the
pattern to the online surface.

**R13-7 (verified, fixed this round):** the acceptance matrix is ~14%
consumer-fidelity (7 of 49 rows classify FIDELITY; 14 CONSISTENCY, 15
SHAPE, 5 METRIC, 5 GATE-MECHANICS, 2 deferred, 1 mixed), and the
oc/px/ac namespace counts were RANGE checks — a dropped row inside the
range passed silently (only core==30 was exact; gates.rs:1175-1192).
D37 made ai exact-count from birth; this round's fix commit tightens
oc/px/ac to exact counts and grows the xtask canary with a dropped-oc
adversarial variant (fails red on the pre-fix range check — the
variant is the fail-without-it evidence).

**R13-8 (verified, recorded):** no golden anywhere pins a tool RESULT
payload — openapi.json/mcp-tools.json are request-side, responses
stubbed "OK" (xtask/src/main.rs:1253-1264). Decision: result payloads
are pinned by behavior contracts (ai1/D39), not goldens; a golden
would pin serialization, not consumer adequacy, and the D35 class
would survive it.

**Hazard sweep (clean):** the D36 process-global-cwd class has exactly
one site in the workspace (supervisor.rs:495-501, serialized since
D36); no other `set_current_dir`/`env::set_var`/`env::remove_var` in
crates/.

